publish a Channel (TvContractCompat.Channels + PreviewPrograms) from inside your existing app, separate from any home-screen UI work. No HomeScreen.tsx changes needed at all — this is additive, one-time registration code.

Key point that makes this easy: writing your own app's channel/program data needs no special permission — the READ_EPG_DATA blocker from earlier only applies to reading other apps' data. Publishing your own is wide open on your current non-rooted box.

And "not a live widget" is exactly right — a channel card is just a static poster image + a deep link, not a rendered AppWidgetHostView. Whatever launcher is active (your own, or stock Google TV if you ever switch back) will pick it up and pin it to the home screen automatically.
1. FileProvider setup (required — a private-storage file needs an authority to hand out a readable content:// URI):

xml
<!-- AndroidManifest.xml -->
<provider
    android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
    android:authorities="com.widgetlauncher.fileprovider"
    android:exported="false"
    android:grantUriPermissions="true">
    <meta-data
        android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
        android:resource="@xml/file_paths" />
</provider>
xml
<!-- res/xml/file_paths.xml -->
<paths>
    <files-path name="widget_snapshots" path="widget_snapshots/" />
</paths>

2. Capture the widget view to a bitmap and save it:

kotlin
fun captureWidgetSnapshot(context: Context, widgetView: View, widgetId: Int): Uri {
    val bitmap = Bitmap.createBitmap(widgetView.width, widgetView.height, Bitmap.Config.ARGB_8888)
    Canvas(bitmap).also { widgetView.draw(it) }

    val dir = File(context.filesDir, "widget_snapshots").apply { mkdirs() }
    val file = File(dir, "widget_$widgetId.png")
    FileOutputStream(file).use { out -> bitmap.compress(Bitmap.CompressFormat.PNG, 100, out) }

    val uri = FileProvider.getUriForFile(context, "com.widgetlauncher.fileprovider", file)

    // Whichever app renders posterArtUri needs read access — grant it explicitly.
    // Your own package always needs it; add the stock launcher too in case you 
    // ever switch off your custom one.
    context.grantUriPermission(context.packageName, uri, Intent.FLAG_GRANT_READ_URI_PERMISSION)
    context.grantUriPermission("com.google.android.tvlauncher", uri, Intent.FLAG_GRANT_READ_URI_PERMISSION)

    return uri
}

3. Refreshing over time: since these are live feeds, a one-time snapshot goes stale. Two options:

Hook into whatever update signal you already get from the AppWidgetHost listener (widget content-changed callback) and re-capture + re-publish right then, or
Periodic refresh via WorkManager (e.g. every few minutes) if the widgets don't reliably fire update callbacks.

Either way, don't insert() a new PreviewProgram each refresh — that'll pile up duplicate cards. Keep the row ID (or a stable internalProviderId you set when first inserting) and update() in place:

kotlin
fun refreshWidgetCardPoster(context: Context, programId: Long, newPosterUri: Uri) {
    val values = ContentValues().apply {
        put(TvContractCompat.PreviewPrograms.COLUMN_POSTER_ART_URI, newPosterUri.toString())
    }
    val uri = TvContractCompat.buildPreviewProgramUri(programId)
    context.contentResolver.update(uri, values, null, null)
}

Store the returned program ID from your original insert() call (parse it with ContentUris.parseId()) alongside each widget's config so you know which row to update on refresh.
